{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>ויאמר בילדכן את העבריות.</b> Whoever is of the family of Eber is called עברי, and after his faith he is called עברי — therefore 'the father of all the children of Eber' (Genesis 10:21) — one who comes from across (מעבר) the river; עבר הנהר is from both sides of the river." "Ben Karish said that the alef in אבנים is added to the root בנים, and the meaning is 'the one who delivers,' like מי אפסים (Ezekiel 47:3). But the correct view is that it is like אבנים, and the alef is a root letter." "והמתן אותו: in secret, so that the matter is not known, for he was doing violence." "And the word וחיה is very difficult in grammar, and it is strange, for the proper form would be וחיתה, for the heh of the root of חיה they would exchange for a tav according to the rule of construct forms, as in 'ולכל חית הארץ' (Genesis 1:30). This is so because the heh and the tav are close in writing, and there is no difference between them except for the prolongation of the vowel point within the heh, but not in pronunciation — for were it not for the prolongation, there would be no difference between them in names: for at first it would be read heh, and when the vowel point is prolonged it is read tav. And this is a sign that the script in our hands is Hebrew script. And the general principle: the word חי, and likewise חיים, is in doubt, for the masters of grammar agreed that 'ויחי ארפכשד' (Genesis 11:12) is of the doubled-root verbs, and like it 'כי אם תם הכסף' (Genesis 47:18), which is from תמם, and חיי is like רבים חיים. But in my opinion this derivation is strange, and if it is not so, then we see from it that it is like a future form, as in אחוג יחוג לחוג — it should have said אֶחוי יחוי לחוי, or like 'סוב,' 'סובו ציון' (Psalms 48:13), 'סבי עיר' (Isaiah 23:16). And behold, this is a sign that it belongs to the verbs with final heh, since we have not found from the root חי a single instance among all the roots we have mentioned. Now this is a sign, that it belongs to the verbs with final heh, for we find that it says אחיה, נחיה, יחיה, תחיה, just as from היה it says נהיה, אהיה, תהיה, יהיה. If so, it is from the root חיה, like היה, even though we find that it says ויחי ארפכשד — in the Hebrew language we need not worry about all this, even though it appears to be of the doubled-root verbs. And I needed to say this because I saw that there is no vav in all of Scripture that is a root letter at the end of a word, and there is no argument against me from the word 'שלו' (Job 16:12), for the vav is in place of the heh, since the letters אהו״י interchange one with another — and the proof: 'שלו כל בוגדי בגד' (Jeremiah 12:1), and it is from שלה. Now I shall explain to you why a root vav does not come at the end of a word. This is because the vav serves at the end to mark the singular possessor, as in עבדו ואמתו (Exodus 20:17), and as a sign of plural in 'פרו ורבו' (Genesis 1:22), and there is also an added vav, as in בנו בעור (Numbers 24:3). And if the vav were a root letter, it would intermix with one of these three vavs we have mentioned — singular, plural, and added — and you would not be able to identify the word. Likewise I tell you regarding the letter yod, that it does not come as a root at the end, for it is a sign of singular speaker: יָדִי (Genesis 42:37), also יָדָי (Deuteronomy 9:15), and a sign of plural in construct: 'ידי נשים רחמניות' (Lamentations 4:10), and a sign of feminine: 'דעי וראי' (1 Samuel 25:17), and the yod of relationship: העברי or העבריה (Deuteronomy 15:12), and also added in verbs: המגביהי (Psalms 113:5), להושיבי (ibid.), and also in nouns: 'בני אתונו' (Genesis 49:11), and in particles of emphasis: זולתי (Deuteronomy 1:36), מני אפרים (Judges 5:14). And because of all this, it is not fitting that a root yod come at the end, lest the word intermix for us with one of all these we have mentioned. Therefore I said that the word וחיה is strange, and it is like וחיתה, and is of the verbs with final heh, as I explained."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra refutes Ben Karish's derivation of אבנים (here in the phrase האבנים, referring to the Hebrew midwives) from a root meaning 'to deliver'; he argues the alef is root, i.e., the word is simply אבן ('stone') in the feminine plural."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:07.575075453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}